00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:12.099
There were a number of items

00:03:12.099 --> 00:03:14.749
that were supposed to come in after the Star
Wars Holiday Special.

00:03:14.749 --> 00:03:17.109
There were action figures based on the wookies

00:03:17.109 --> 00:03:19.240
that formed a big part of the holiday special.

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:22.340
There were, you know, books on the wookies,

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:25.340
I think a lot of that was scaled back when
it came out.

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:27.720
But no, Lucasfilm thought that was going to
be big.

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:30.940
If you look at the original scripts for the
holiday special,

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:33.560
it was going to be a bit better than it turned
out,

00:03:33.569 --> 00:03:36.549
it just ended up in the hands of a couple
variety producers

00:03:36.549 --> 00:03:39.890
who made it way more cheesy than it was intended,

00:03:39.890 --> 00:03:42.700
flipped the story around so it was basically
unwatchable.

00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:45.320
Speaking of unwatchable, how about all those
prequels?

00:03:45.320 --> 00:03:46.760
Don't they count as failures too?

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:48.180
Meesa no think so.

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:49.900
They were all profitable.

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:51.379
Someone went to see them.

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:54.200
Episode One was the one that has made the
most money.

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:59.540
In pure dollar terms, yes, un-adjusted for
inflation, Episode One made more money.

00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:02.200
Interestingly it made most of it's money abroad,

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:06.140
so consider the fact that what most people
hated about the prequels was,

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:07.780
you know, the stilted dialogue.

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:10.460
Anakin, chancellor Palpatine is evil.

00:04:10.460 --> 00:04:13.120
From my point of view the Jedi are evil.

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:14.280
The lack of good acting.

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:19.540
None of these things can you see if you're
watching it internationally

00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:22.760
if it's dubbed, if it's, or you're looking
at subtitles.

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:26.240
That may sound like good luck but it speaks
to the innovation of the franchise.

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:29.140
Innovation it developed after the first movie
hit theaters,

00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:31.700
back when few people thought Star Wars would
be a hit,

00:04:31.700 --> 00:04:35.040
and even fewer thought it would make money
on, of all things, merchandising.

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:37.060
Movie merchandising did not make money,

00:04:37.060 --> 00:04:41.480
it worked for TV franchises like Star Trek,
it was not expected to work for Star Wars.

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:44.160
And it almost didn't, the process of making
toys took so long

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:47.500
that Kenner ended up selling cardboard versions
of their toys at Christmas time

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:49.400
with the promise that they would arrive later.

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:52.600
They'll be sent to you at home between February
first and June first.

00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:56.180
The Star Wars early bird certificate package,
new from Kenner.

00:04:56.180 --> 00:04:59.320
You know, they took so long to ramp up their
production

00:04:59.320 --> 00:05:02.260
that by the time the toys actually arrived
in the stores,

00:05:02.260 --> 00:05:05.740
supposedly we thought Star Wars would be out
of theaters.

00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:07.740
Of course as it turned out it was there for
a year.

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:09.620
So what was different about Star Wars,

00:05:09.620 --> 00:05:12.560
how did it change the movie industry as far
as merchandising goes?

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:14.280
Because it stuck around for so long

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:17.960
it actually meant that you could get the merchandising
to consumers

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:21.840
while the movie was still in the theaters,
which was huge,

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:24.040
and that had not been done before.

00:05:24.040 --> 00:05:25.320
Sort of because of the fans.

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:29.460
It was because of the fans, it was because
there was this incredible repeat-ability to it

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:33.120
and there was a cult, almost immediately.

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:37.700
There were local entrepreneurs who came along
and made T shirts.

00:05:37.700 --> 00:05:40.920
This was the era of making buttons, right?

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:43.400
Making buttons at home, the little button
entrepreneurs,

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:48.020
so immediately you 'may the force be with
you' became a button.

00:05:48.020 --> 00:05:50.860
And that was very much a sort of, you know,
oh do you understand this?

00:05:50.860 --> 00:05:52.920
you're in with the hip crowd.

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:55.460
What about the underground merchandising,
you mentioned buttons,

00:05:55.460 --> 00:05:59.540
but also people made their own costumes, people
made their own fan-fiction,

00:05:59.540 --> 00:06:04.000
people made things on their own because they
were fans of what was going on,

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:05.380
whether they wanted to sell it or not.

00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:08.160
And Lucasfilm really wanted to encourage that
at the start.

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:13.060
The only part of the fan, the traditional
fan fiction side of things

00:06:13.060 --> 00:06:20.440
that they looked askance at was the slash
fiction, which is basically fan written porn.

00:06:20.440 --> 00:06:23.480
This had been huge in the Star Trek community,

00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:28.160
but otherwise, other than that, they used
the power of the fans right from the beginning.

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:30.539
That power's on full display from the R2 Builders,

00:06:30.540 --> 00:06:34.080
a group of amateur droid engineers who make
their own R2D2s

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:35.380
and share their work online.

00:06:35.380 --> 00:06:40.199
But they tend to spend, on average, I'd say
about 10 years on each R2D2

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:42.260
I mean it is so meticulous.

00:06:42.260 --> 00:06:45.120
They're in their garages, you know, honing
their metal domes

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:47.620
and just trying to get every detail accurate.

00:06:47.620 --> 00:06:52.720
But in so doing and in being so obsessed with
that goal and tinkering with the R2D2s

00:06:52.730 --> 00:06:57.120
they became so much better than Lucasfilm
at building an R2D2.

00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:02.110
So they were actually tapped by Kathleen Kennedy,
the president of Lucasfilm,

00:07:02.110 --> 00:07:06.560
to create the R2 that will show in The Force
Awakens.

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:11.580
It's, again, you know, treat your fans well,
you get this tremendous,

00:07:11.580 --> 00:07:15.700
not quite unpaid labor, but you know, you
get a little skunk-works.

00:07:15.700 --> 00:07:18.000
What kind of Star Wars do you think we'll
expect from Disney?

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:23.199
I think Disney is very very smart in the way
that it approaches it's subsidiaries,

00:07:23.199 --> 00:07:25.449
if you look at the way it treated Pixar-

00:07:25.449 --> 00:07:27.410
Which was originally owned by?

00:07:27.410 --> 00:07:30.260
George Lucas, still calls it 'my company.'

00:07:30.260 --> 00:07:32.620
But Pixar was treated like crown jewels,

00:07:32.620 --> 00:07:35.000
they could make whatever they wanted to make,
the same is true of Marvel.

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:37.780
We almost don't think of Marvel as a Disney
subsidiary.

00:07:37.780 --> 00:07:41.020
And brilliantly the same hands off approach
has been taken to Lucasfilm.

00:07:41.020 --> 00:07:43.810
There's very much a 'it ain't broke so don't
fix it.'

00:07:43.810 --> 00:07:47.920
Let the nerds who are in charge of the franchise
stay in charge.

00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:51.940
Let the guys in the story group figure out
where all the bits of the universe go.

00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:07.520
You know I think Episode Seven has the distinct
potential

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:09.840
to be the highest grossing film of all time.

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:14.580
And I think the interesting thing about this
is it all feeds into the other parts of the empire.

00:08:14.580 --> 00:08:16.760
It's more of a success than The Force Awakens
is,

00:08:16.760 --> 00:08:19.020
the more of a success the merchandising will
be.

00:08:19.020 --> 00:08:21.200
You know you just referred to Disney as 'the
Empire?'

00:08:22.660 --> 00:08:27.920
They are, I mean it's, you know, but then
Lucasfilm saw itself as the empire as well

00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:30.860
I mean Lucas would often refer to himself
as Darth Vader

00:08:30.860 --> 00:08:35.000
and the Lucasfilm headquarters was known as
the Death Star.

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:39.820
But yes, when the Mouse House moved in, I
mean that is a real empire.

00:08:39.820 --> 00:08:41.060
No question about that.

